# Fixtures and Test Data

## Author Fixtures in tests/Fixture

A fixture is a `TestFixture` subclass whose `$records` are inserted before each test. Keep every record's keys identical — rows are bulk-inserted — and turn on `strictFields` so typos and schema drift fail loudly instead of silently:

```php
namespace App\Test\Fixture;

use Cake\TestSuite\Fixture\TestFixture;

class ArticlesFixture extends TestFixture
{
    protected bool $strictFields = true;

    public array $records = [
        [
            'title' => 'First Article',
            'body' => 'First Article Body',
            'published' => '1',
            'created' => '2007-03-18 10:39:23',
            'modified' => '2007-03-18 10:41:31',
        ],
    ];
}
```

Need functions or dynamic data in rows? Build `$records` in `init()` instead of the property. Do not hand-write auto-increment values — they interfere with sequence generation on PostgreSQL and SQL Server. Keep fixture schema aligned with the migrations, and match multi-datasource models with `test_`-prefixed fixture connections (`test_mydb` for `mydb`), so a run can never truncate application data.

## Each Test Declares and Owns Its Data

Load a fixture for every model the test queries, in each test case that needs it:

```php
protected array $fixtures = ['app.Articles', 'app.Comments'];
```

Use `getFixtures()` for a method-defined list, FQCN entries (`ArticlesFixture::class`), plugin fixtures (`'plugin.DebugKit.Articles'`), core fixtures (`'core.Comments'`), or subdirectories (`'app.Blog/Articles'` → `tests/Fixture/Blog/`).

{% if pest %}
Create mutable rows inside the test that uses them, or through a helper the test calls. Use `beforeEach()` only for configuration that applies to every test in the file. Do not create records in it.
{% else %}
Create mutable rows inside each test or through a private helper that the test calls. Use `setUp()` only for configuration that applies to every test in the class. Do not create records in it, because its objects remain in memory until the suite ends.
{% endif %}

Create only the rows required to arrange the behavior or support an assertion, and declare each row's owner explicitly. Prefer the rolled-back-transaction strategy per class when tests do not depend on resetting auto-increment between methods:

```php
protected function getFixtureStrategy(): FixtureStrategyInterface
{
    return new TransactionStrategy();
}
```

When static `$records` grow hard to maintain, generate scenarios with the fixture-factories plugin (`bin/cake bake fixture_factory`).

## Data Providers

Use a data provider when the setup, test body, and assertions remain the same across input values.

```php
public static function nonAdminRoles(): array
{
    return [
        'editor' => ['editor'],
        'viewer' => ['viewer'],
    ];
}

#[DataProvider('nonAdminRoles')]
public function test_forbids_roles_other_than_admin(string $role): void
{
    // ... arrange user with $role, request the endpoint, assert refusal ...
}
```

Declare each data provider method as `public static`.

Use parameterized tests for:

- roles and permissions
- boundary values
- input values that are invalid in the same way
- input and output value pairs

Write separate tests if the cases need a different setup, a different behavior, or different assertions. One test method with a branch in the body is two tests in one method.

Give each data-provider case a key that states the difference. A failure then identifies the case without requiring you to count positions.
